section 3 has said to resolve the family from the registry the whole
time. All of them now route through one predicate.

THE RULING THE OWNER DELEGATED, AND IT INVERTS THE ITEM'S PREMISE.

The item asked whether `includes` belongs to the family, describing it as
the Ruby mixin edge type. It is not one relationship. `includes` is a
REGISTERED TWO-MEANING VALUE -- its own entry reads "File or class
includes / sources / mixes-in another unit's ..." -- emitted by nine
producers of which EIGHT mean file inclusion: latex, make, meson, puppet,
requirements, rst, scss, twig.

Measured on postal: 41 `includes` edges = 39 class->module Ruby mixins
plus 2 file->external_symbol edges from an SCSS @include. Enrolling the
bare string, which is the obvious reading of the item, would have made a
stylesheet @include and a Makefile include into class is-a edges inside
slice expansion, ranking centrality and the dead-code ancestor walk --
the mirror-image mistake the item itself warned about, reached by
following the item's own framing.

So the family is a PREDICATE OVER THE EDGE, not a set of type strings.
is_inheritance_edge() discriminates on the already-registered
evidence_type="ast_includes" ("Edge inferred from a runtime mixin
declaration"), which exactly one emit site produces. A test pins the SCSS
case as producing NO dispatch, so a future widening cannot pass quietly.

MEASURED, AND IT IS NOT UNIFORM. postal: type-hierarchy dispatches
53 -> 58. openzeppelin-contracts: ZERO delta across 516 inherits edges,
because a second cause on a different axis was waiting behind the first
-- solidity emits contract members as kind=function (2,025 members of
type-like containers, none kind=method) while link_type_hierarchy indexes
with a literal sym.kind != "method". The edge now reaches the dispatch
map and finds it empty. Filed as INV-lapas, not folded in. A test pins
the still-broken Solidity shape so this cannot read as a claim it works.

INDEPENDENT CLOSURE EVIDENCE: test_interface_dispatch_parity's ruby cell
was a STRICT xfail naming this item by ID. It now XPASSes -- a
pre-existing gate written by someone else flipped green. Retired it; the
